      "a": "Summary\nSummary: Mongo insert a todo. insertOne with text, done, userId. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `await db.collection(\"todos\").insertOne(doc` — Create. A line you can point at: async function createTodo(userId, text) { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. One shared register. Unique emails. Reports that do not guess. Interviewers want you to say WHERE first, then the join, then the risk of no WHERE. DELETE/UPDATE without WHERE. String-concat SQL. Running a 20-second report on the checkout primary. NULL is not empty string. Name Create/Read/Update/Delete if this is a list, or 401 vs 403 if this is login.\n\nThe problem before\nSpreadsheets on one laptop, or UPDATE without WHERE, or a report that locked checkout. People glued user text into SQL and opened an injection hole. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. insertOne with text, done, userId. DELETE/UPDATE without WHERE. String-concat SQL. Running a 20-second report on the checkout primary. NULL is not empty string.\n\nWhat this is\ninsertOne with text, done, userId. SQL talks to tables with rules. INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE. WHERE vs HAVING. JOIN combines tables. Parameters ($1 or ?) stop injection. Transactions keep two writes together. Walk the snippet like this: `await db.collection(\"todos\").insertOne(doc` — Create. One shared register. Unique emails. Reports that do not guess. Interviewers want you to say WHERE first, then the join, then the risk of no WHERE. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nWhat it solves\nOne shared register. Unique emails. Reports that do not guess. Interviewers want you to say WHERE first, then the join, then the risk of no WHERE. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). insertOne with text, done, userId. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA school register. One row per student. You cannot mark roll 99 if 99 is not in the book. Changing marks without a roll number would rewrite the whole class. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. Mongo insert a todo. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nUsers, orders, money, analytics. PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: Mongo insert a todo. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. One shared register. Unique emails. Reports that do not guess. Interviewers want you to say WHERE first, then the join, then the risk of no WHERE.\n\nWatch out\nDELETE/UPDATE without WHERE. String-concat SQL. Running a 20-second report on the checkout primary. NULL is not empty string. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. insertOne with text, done, userId. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.",
