      "q": "Practice: React TodoList",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: React TodoList. useState + useEffect load. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]` — list on the screen. `.then(setTodos` — Read. A line you can point at: function TodoList() { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. The right row keeps its textbox. Timers do not thrash the tree. Interviewers want this split in one breath. key={index} breaks on reorder. key={Math.random()} remounts every paint. Do not put visible text only in a ref. Name Create/Read/Update/Delete if this is a list, or 401 vs 403 if this is login.\n\nThe problem before\nIndex keys mixed input state after a delete. Timer ids in useState caused extra paints. Lists remounted because keys were random. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. useState + useEffect load. key={index} breaks on reorder. key={Math.random()} remounts every paint. Do not put visible text only in a ref.\n\nWhat this is\nuseState + useEffect load. useState is a value that repaints the screen. useRef.current does not repaint. key must be a stable id so React can match rows after insert/delete. Map data to components; do not copy the DOM by hand. Walk the snippet like this: `const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]` — list on the screen. `.then(setTodos` — Read. The right row keeps its textbox. Timers do not thrash the tree. Interviewers want this split in one breath.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe right row keeps its textbox. Timers do not thrash the tree. Interviewers want this split in one breath. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). useState + useEffect load. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nName stickers on lunch trays, not 1-2-3 in line. If Ada leaves, Bob does not inherit her pickle. The pickle is component state. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. React TodoList. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nTodo UIs, forms, lists, focus, intervals. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: React TodoList. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. The right row keeps its textbox. Timers do not thrash the tree. Interviewers want this split in one breath. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nWatch out\nkey={index} breaks on reorder. key={Math.random()} remounts every paint. Do not put visible text only in a ref. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. useState + useEffect load. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.",
      "code": "function TodoList() {\n  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);  // list on the screen\n  useEffect(function () {\n    api(\"/api/todos\")\n      .then(function (res) { return res.json(); })\n      .then(setTodos);  // Read\n  }, []);\n  return todos.map((t) => <p key={t._id}>{t.text}</p>);\n}"
