      "id": 5,
      "level": "intermediate",
      "q": "Practice: React add form",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: React add form. preventDefault, POST, clear input. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `e.preventDefault` — do not reload the page. `body: JSON.stringify({ text }` — Create. `setText(\"\"` — clear the box. A line you can point at: async function onSubmit(e, text, setText) { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Later steps can find this row by id. Tick, edit, and delete become map/filter or UPDATE/DELETE WHERE id. Interviewers want the word Create from CRUD, plus validation before you push. Do not push empty strings. Do not use the array index as a lasting id if you will delete the middle item. Date.now is fine for a lab; a database uses a real primary key. Never trust the client to pick a privileged field like role: 'admin'.\n\nThe problem before\nIf you only keep a string on the screen, you cannot tick, edit, or delete that one item later. Two identical titles collide. Refresh wipes the list because nothing was stored as a real record with an id. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. preventDefault, POST, clear input.\n\nWhat this is\npreventDefault, POST, clear input. Create means you build one object that represents the thing, give it a unique id, then add it to the list or table. Empty text is not a row. Trim spaces first. Return the new object or a 201 body so the UI can paint it. Walk the snippet like this: `e.preventDefault` — do not reload the page. `body: JSON.stringify({ text }` — Create. `setText(\"\"` — clear the box.\n\nWhat it solves\nLater steps can find this row by id. Tick, edit, and delete become map/filter or UPDATE/DELETE WHERE id. Interviewers want the word Create from CRUD, plus validation before you push. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). preventDefault, POST, clear input. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA school attendance book. You do not shout 'Ada is here' into the air. You write one new line: roll number, name, present. That line is the object. The roll number is the id. A blank name is not a line. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet.\n\nUses\nTodo add, signup, POST /todos, INSERT, 'Add card' on a form. Any button that should make a new row. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: React add form. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Later steps can find this row by id. Tick, edit, and delete become map/filter or UPDATE/DELETE WHERE id. Interviewers want the word Create from CRUD, plus validation before you push.\n\nWatch out\nDo not push empty strings. Do not use the array index as a lasting id if you will delete the middle item. Date.now is fine for a lab; a database uses a real primary key. Never trust the client to pick a privileged field like role: 'admin'. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview.",
      "code": "async function onSubmit(e, text, setText) {\n  e.preventDefault();  // do not reload the page\n  await api(\"/api/todos\", {\n    method: \"POST\",\n    body: JSON.stringify({ text })  // Create\n  });\n  setText(\"\");  // clear the box\n}"
