      "q": "Practice: Mongo unique email error",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: Mongo unique email error. Catch 11000 and send 409. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `e.status = 409` — already exists. A line you can point at: async function createUser(email, hash) { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. You get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo. Do not skip validation. Do not leave secrets in the snippet. If the lab is client-only, say that the real server must still check. Name Create/Read/Update/Delete if this is a list, or 401 vs 403 if this is login.\n\nThe problem before\nThe feature was only a comment in someone's head. The page looked done until a refresh, a second user, or a wrong id. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. Catch 11000 and send 409. Do not skip validation. Do not leave secrets in the snippet. If the lab is client-only, say that the real server must still check.\n\nWhat this is\nCatch 11000 and send 409. This lab is one small complete job. Read the function name, then each comment. Copy it, run it, hide it, write it again. That is how the sheet is built. Walk the snippet like this: `e.status = 409` — already exists. You get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). Catch 11000 and send 409. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA cooking class card: one dish, one method. You do not open five cookbooks. You follow this card, then the next. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. Mongo unique email error. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nEvery practice row on this page. Same pattern in frontend, API, SQL, and cloud. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: Mongo unique email error. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. You get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo.\n\nWatch out\nDo not skip validation. Do not leave secrets in the snippet. If the lab is client-only, say that the real server must still check. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. Catch 11000 and send 409.",
      "code": "async function createUser(email, hash) {\n  try {\n    await db.collection(\"users\").insertOne({ email, hash });\n  } catch (err) {\n    if (err.code === 11000) {\n      const e = new Error(\"email taken\");\n      e.status = 409;  // already exists\n      throw e;\n    }\n    throw err;\n  }\n}"
